---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout title="Playing Through Hate - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Playing Through Hate"
        description="Mastering post-sideboard matches requires understanding how to navigate around targeted opposing interaction. You do not need an explicit answer to every sideboard threat; rather, winning Games 2 and 3 relies on altering your sequencing, timing your stack triggers, and executing disciplined counterplay."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/decree.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Decree of Silence"
        imageCaption="Navigating Tournament Disruption"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Graveyard Interference" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
            Because <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> relies heavily on the graveyard to cheat massive mana costs, opponents will inevitably present graveyard disruption in post-board games. The secret to defeating graveyard hate is simple: never commit critical resources blindly without an immediate win condition on the stack or in hand.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Withered Wretch"
            image="creatures/withered_wretch.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>(Black Aggro, The Rock):</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a> is exceptionally dangerous because its graveyard exile activation is repeatable, instant-speed, and attached to an aggressive creature body that clocks your life total.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> When playing against black midrange or aggro, strictly preserve your copies of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares">Swords to Plowshares</a> specifically for Withered Wretch. Do not waste interaction on redundant aggressive beaters like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Jackal Pup">Jackal Pup</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Llanowar Elves">Llanowar Elves</a> if doing so leaves you helpless against a Wretch. Prioritize removing it before activating card selection engines or committing enchantments to your graveyard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Universal Exile:</strong> The simplest physical answer to an early <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a> is destroying it with <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a> before initiating a combo loop. However, relying purely on finding removal is undisciplined; knowing how to out-sequence Crypt on the stack is a foundational Replenish skill.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The Stack Trick:</strong> Against an active Crypt on the battlefield, do not blindly dump your entire hand into your graveyard. Instead, maintain an active <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> on the table while retaining your winning combo pieces in hand. With a moderate, tempting graveyard (just enough value to bait activation), cast <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>. When your opponent sacrifices Crypt in response to purge your existing yard, Replenish remains sitting on the stack. Still in response to your own Replenish, activate your Attunement to dump a fresh hand of combo enchantments into your newly cleared graveyard. Replenish resolves instantly thereafter, returning everything you just discarded.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Phyrexian Furnace"
            image="artifacts/phyrexian_furnace.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Incremental Attrition:</strong> Unlike <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a> exiles only one card at a time via targeted sacrifice or random tap activations.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Never commit your combo enchantments to the graveyard gradually over several turns—this simply invites your opponent to snipe key lock pieces one by one. Instead, execute your setup explosively in a single turn via <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a> immediately before casting <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>. By overwhelming their single targeted exile activation on the stack, they can capture at most one card while the rest of your architecture returns to end the game.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Haunting Echoes"
            image="sorceries/haunting_echoes.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Graveyard & Library Extraction:</strong> The supreme terminal hate piece of Black-based control shells and Psychatog variants. Resolving a five-mana <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Haunting Echoes">Haunting Echoes</a> obliterates your accumulated graveyard while scouring your entire library of all namesake copies, completely disabling your combo architecture.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Because Haunting Echoes is a heavy five-mana sorcery, opponents are forced to tap down significantly during their own main phase to deploy it. Preserve cheap blue countermagic (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell">Counterspell</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial">Arcane Denial</a>) specifically for their fifth mana threshold, or proactively lock out the spell via <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a> in Games 2 and 3. Avoid passively building an overflowing graveyard against five-mana black control pools without an immediate plan to execute Replenish on your own turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Enchantment Sweepers & Removal" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
            When opponents fail to attack your graveyard, they frequently overload on reactive anti-enchantment removal to dismantle your engines once they reach the battlefield.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tranquil Domain / Naturalize"
            image="instants/tranquil_domain.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Instant-Speed Destruction:</strong> Traditional green answers aimed at destroying your lock pieces as they are cast or attacking your established board during combat or end steps.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a> serves as the premier overarching defense: returning Decree alongside your combo forces the opponent to exhaust three spells just to break through the automatic counter shielding. Furthermore, under an active Opal-Wave engine, you can activate <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> targeting your own vulnerable enchantments in response to removal; once the stack clears and Wave triggers, your board returns unharmed and refreshed. Finally, transitioning into our post-sideboard creature package (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a>) completely invalidates opposing naturalize suites by presenting lethal damage from non-enchantment sources.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Scour"
            image="instants/scour.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Surgical Eradication:</strong> Primarily fielded by blue-white control sideboards utilizing Invasion-block removal suites, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Scour">Scour</a> threatens to destroy an enchantment on the battlefield and permanently exile all corresponding copies across your hand, library, and graveyard. A single resolved Scour targeting <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> can dismantle your foundational win condition for the rest of the match.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Never deploy an unprotected engine enchantment against open four-mana white suites post-sideboard without permission or a defensive plan. Most importantly, when initiating an Opal-Wave loop, maintain active fading counters on your Parallax Wave so you can instantly exile your own enchantments in response to Scour targeting them on the stack. If the target leaves the battlefield before resolution, Scour completely fizzles and zero copies are exiled.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Anarchy"
            image="sorceries/anarchy.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Red Sweeper & Color Hate:</strong> Aggressive red architectures (Sligh, RG Ponza, Goblins) consistently side in <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a> to destroy white stabilization engines like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a>, and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> in a single sweeping blow.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Anarchy is a four-mana sorcery that destroys rather than exiles. First, proactively throttle their mana development via <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a> to keep them off four operational mana, or name it defensively with <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a>. Second, recognize that because Anarchy sends all destroyed enchantments directly to your graveyard, retaining a backup copy of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> allows you to instantly reanimate your entire wiped board on your subsequent turn, turning an apparent catastrophe into immediate game-ending tempo.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Reverent Silence"
            image="sorceries/reverent_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Free Enchantment Sweeper:</strong> Utilized by Elves and green aggro-midrange builds as zero-mana interaction by having you gain 6 life as an alternate casting cost. While sweeping your entire enchantment board for zero mana can ruin early stabilization pieces like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a>, the dynamic is deeply advantageous if planned correctly.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> As a sorcery, opponents are restricted to casting <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Reverent Silence">Reverent Silence</a> during their main phase. During preliminary setup rounds, gladly accept the 6-life cushion; every destroyed enchantment lands directly in your graveyard, actively advancing your <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> setup at zero card cost to you. Once your Opal-Wave engine is fully assembled, simply activate <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> in response to stack self-protection triggers, bringing your entire board back safely once Reverent Silence resolves.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Taxing Structures & Lockouts" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
            Static restriction permanents attempt to lock down your resource development or prevent your core engine loops from executing cleanly.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Presence of the Master"
            image="enchantments/presence_of_the_master.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>The Replenish Bypass:</strong> While imposing on paper against an enchantment archetype, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Presence of the Master">Presence of the Master</a> strictly counters enchantment spells that are <em>played</em> (cast from your hand). <strong>It does not intercept enchantments entering the battlefield via <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>!</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                However, a resolved Presence prevents you from casting manual combo pieces or hardcasting answers like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a>. To navigate around it, focus entirely on sculpting a decisive graveyard and winning directly off a single Replenish, or rely on instant-speed removal like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a> and non-enchantment proactive threats like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a>.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Aura of Silence"
            image="enchantments/aura_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Tax & Threat Combination:</strong> Frequently sideboarded in White Weenie, BW Control, and Enchantress mirrors. Because <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a> increases the casting cost of your artifacts and enchantments by two generic mana, deploying early ramp or hardcasting combo engines becomes agonizingly slow. Worse still, sitting openly on the battlefield, it represents an immediate sacrifice trigger to destroy whatever key enchantment you do manage to resolve.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> This interaction underscores precisely why our sideboard engineers a split between <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a>. Disenchant remains a clean two-mana instant entirely immune to Aura's static tax. In attrition matchups where Aura hits the table early, concentrate your mana on resolving <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a> to load the graveyard, then cast a multi-enchantment <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>; a single sacrifice trigger cannot dismantle a complete Opal-Wave-Decree lock entering simultaneously.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Gloom"
            image="matchups/moneyball/gloom.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Asymmetric Color Tax:</strong> Brought in by Black aggro, Suicide Black, and The Rock. By taxing all white spells and white enchantment activation costs by three generic mana, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a> threatens to cripple <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares">Swords to Plowshares</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a>, and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Fortunately, Gloom is strictly color-scoped to white. Your entire blue card-selection engine (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Deep Analysis">Deep Analysis</a>) and blue permission suite (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell">Counterspell</a>) function entirely unimpeded. Against heavily black fields, reserve early blue countermagic strictly for intercepting Gloom on the stack. If resolved, leverage sol lands (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="City of Traitors">City of Traitors</a>) and artifact ramp to manually overcome the three-mana surcharge over extended games, or rely on blue bounce mechanics like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Upheaval">Upheaval</a> and cycling <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a>.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Winter Orb"
            image="artifacts/winter_orb.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Mana Chokepoints:</strong> Featured heavily in Stasis, Merfolk, and aggressive tempo builds. Because Replenish operates around a critical three- and four-mana spell curve, restricted untap steps severely constrain your operational bandwidth and setup speed.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Prioritize removing <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Winter Orb">Winter Orb</a> immediately with <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a> or instant-speed <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a>. In game lines where Orb persists, sol lands like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a> provide immense tactical leverage by generating two mana off a single untap step. Most importantly, remember that <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a> functions as an extraordinary burst untapper under Winter Orb: tap all available lands for mana before casting Search, then untap three lands upon resolution to suddenly generate a massive mana pool during a choked turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Cursed Totem"
            image="artifacts/cursed_totem.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>The Opalescence Lockout:</strong> A subtle and deceptively dangerous sideboard artifact. On an empty board or during standard setup phases, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Cursed Totem">Cursed Totem</a> has zero interaction with normal enchantments. However, <strong>the instant <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a> enters the battlefield, all of your non-Aura enchantments transform into creatures.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                Under Opalescence and Cursed Totem simultaneously, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a>, and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a> are strictly considered creatures with activated abilities—meaning Cursed Totem completely disables their activation loops! To circumvent this critical gotcha, always utilize <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a> or instant-speed <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a> to destroy Cursed Totem <em>before</em> deploying Opalescence onto the battlefield.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Creature Interference" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Precision Bottlenecks:</strong> Opponents fielding blue-white builds (UW Landstill, mirrors) frequently introduce <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a> post-board to lock down your namesake <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> or foundational combo engines like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Maintain vigilant situational awareness regarding which opponent archetypes have access to white and blue mana after Games 1. When anticipating opposing Mages, deliberately retain spot removal suite representation (leaving in 1–2 copies of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares">Swords to Plowshares</a> or relying on <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a>). A single Meddling Mage represents a temporary hurdle rather than a hard lockout if you construct your sideboard transitions to expect it.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
